{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01P5X5L7B1HhThc6g3avLj5r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose the next task.\n\n## Project Summary\n\n**Problem:** Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge in observable behavior. This project derives specs from C source code, generates Go implementations using AI, and verifies parity via differential testing.\n\n**Architecture:**\n- `cmd/` - Per-utility Go implementations\n- `pkg/sys/` - Platform-specific syscall abstractions\n- `pkg/format/` - Output formatting (columns, colors, sizes)\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing harness\n- `magefiles/` - Build targets\n- `docs/` - Specifications (PRDs, use cases, test suites)\n\n\n- `pkg/testutils/` - Differential testing framework with test data and binary harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - Platform abstractions for signals, file stats, and terminal operations across Darwin and Linux\n- `pkg/format/` - Formatting utilities for colors, columnar output, and size representations\n\nLooking at the command implementations, I see cat, wc, sponge, du, true, and version are all present with their test files. The release tracking shows some inconsistency—rel00.0 and rel01.0 are marked as code not started, but the source already contains implementations for these packages and commands, with rel01.1 showing code completion. Looking at the orphaned PRDs, I see that true and false already have specifications (prd020 and prd021) even though rel02.0 is marked as pending. This means I need to account for those existing PRDs when planning the release structure, rather than treating them as completely missing. I need to verify what PRD content is actually available in the project context, since the analysis mentions orphaned PRDs but I don't have the full details. Looking at cmd/true as a reference point, cmd/false would be nearly identical in structure—around 77 lines for main.go and 151 for tests—but exit with status 1 instead of 0. The challenge is that P2 requires splitting implementation and tests into separate tasks, but I can only propose one task right now. I see the contradiction now—\"combine trivial changes\" must mean combining multiple trivial features together, not merging implementation with tests. The rule about splitting those into separate tasks is clear. So I should propose just the cmd/false implementation as a single task, since I can't do both implementation and tests together. I need to anchor this to the PRD requirements, and since cmd/false mirrors cmd/true, I can infer the requirements would be similar—starting with the exit code behavior. I need to map out the requirements for the `false` command based on the pattern from `true`, keeping it to 4 requirements maximum. The key ones are the exit status (1 instead of 0), help and version flags, ignoring extra arguments, and signal/error handling. I'll reference prd021-false as required reading and structure the task to implement cmd/false/main.go as the next priority after cmd/true. R1: Exit with status 1 unconditionally per prd021-false R1\nR2: Handle --help and --version as first argument per prd021-false R2\nR3: Silently ignore all other arguments per prd021-false R3\nR4: SIGPIPE handling and write error exit codes per prd021-false R5\n\nThat's 4 requirements - good.\n\nDesign decisions:\nD1: Follow the same structure as cmd/true/main.go (manual argument parsing, no flag package)\nD2: Use pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler() for SIGPIPE handling\nD3: Use bufio.NewWriter for output buffering\n\nAcceptance criteria:\nAC1: cmd/false/main.go compiles without errors\nAC2: Running cmd/false with no args exits with status 1\nAC3: Running cmd/false --help and --version exits with status 0 and prints help/version info, matching GNU coreutils behavior where these flags take precedence and exit successfully\nAC4: Running cmd/false with any other arguments exits with status 1\nAC5: SIGPIPE errors during write operations cause exit with status 141 (128 + 13)\n\nNow I'm implementing the main function that mirrors true's structure but inverts the default exit code to 1, with --help and --version still exiting 0. Writing the implementation...\n\nNow I need to set up the task properly—I'll reference the PRD for the false command, use the true implementation as a structural guide, and pull in the signal handler utility. The main file to create is cmd/false/main.go, and I need to anchor the requirements to the specific PRD sections covering exit codes, help output, and version handling. I'm setting up the fourth requirement around SIGPIPE handling and write error detection. For the design, I'll follow the same pattern as the true command with manual argument parsing and the bufio writer approach, plus installing the SIGPIPE handler from the sys package with the version variable set at build time. Now I need to verify the acceptance criteria—checking that the binary compiles, runs correctly with no arguments, and handles the various flag combinations properly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":89430,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":89430},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"462c7d97-6a8b-4cbd-afda-e9c6e06e5368","uuid":"63d8b15a-8258-4d68-9a5d-a414f4dd8799"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01P5X5L7B1HhThc6g3avLj5r","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem:** go-unix-utils is an experiment in specification-driven development where Unix utilities are re-implemented in Go from C-source-derived specs and verified via differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture:** Four-stage pipeline (extract → specify → synthesize → verify) with `cmd/` per-utility packages, shared `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `pkg/testutils/` (differential testing harness), and `magefiles/` (build infrastructure).\n\n**Current State (from source_code):** The following packages have working code: `pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/wc`, `cmd/cat`, `cmd/sponge`, `cmd/du`, `cmd/true`, `cmd/version`. This covers rel00.0, rel01.0, rel01.1, rel01.2, rel01.3 implementations and the `true` utility from rel02.0.\n\n**Current Release:** rel02.0 (Batch 2 -- trivial utilities). `cmd/true` already has code. Remaining use cases: `false`, `yes`, `basename`, `dirname`. An orphaned PRD `prd021-false` exists.\n\n## Priority Reasoning\n\nThe earliest incomplete release is rel02.0. Within rel02.0, `cmd/true` already exists (source_code confirms). The next utility in canonical order is `cmd/false` (prd021-false PRD exists). `false` is the mirror of `true` -- exits 1 instead of 0. Per P2, implementation and tests are separate tasks. The implementation task comes first.\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"cmd/false: implement exit-1 utility (prd021-false R1-R3, R5)\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd021-false.yaml (PRD for false utility)\n      - cmd/true/main.go (structural reference -- false mirrors true with exit code 1)\n      - pkg/sys/signal.go (InstallSIGPIPEHandler used for SIGPIPE handling)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/false/main.go\n        action: create\n        note: \"false utility -- exits 1 unconditionally except for --help and --version\"\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: \"Exit with status 1 when invoked with no arguments or with any arguments that are not --help or --version as the first argument, per prd021-false R1\"\n      - id: R2\n        text: \"When --help is the first argument, print a usage message to stdout and exit 0; when --version is the first argument, print version information to stdout and exit 0, per prd021-false R2\"\n      - id: R3\n        text: \"Silently ignore all arguments that are not --help or --version in the first position -- do not emit errors for unknown flags or operands, per prd021-false R3\"\n      - id: R4\n        text: \"Install SIGPIPE handler via pkg/sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler at startup; on stdout write errors in --help or --version, print a diagnostic to stderr and exit 1, per prd021-false R5\"\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: \"Follow cmd/true/main.go structure exactly -- manual argument parsing without the flag package, only the first argument is checked for --help and --version, all other arguments are silently ignored\"\n      - id: D2\n        text: \"Use bufio.NewWriter(os.Stdout) for --help and --version output, flush and check for write errors before exiting\"\n      - id: D3\n        text: \"The version variable is set at build time via -ldflags '-X main.version=<tag>'; default value is 'dev'\"\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: \"cmd/false/main.go compiles without errors via 'go build ./cmd/false/'\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"Running the binary with no arguments exits with status 1 and produces no stdout or stderr output\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: \"Running with --help as the first argument prints usage to stdout and exits 0\"\n      - id: AC4\n        text: \"Running with --version as the first argument prints version to stdout and exits 0\"\n      - id: AC5\n        text: \"Running with arbitrary arguments (e.g., 'foo', '-v', '--unknown') exits with status 1 and produces no output\"\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":89430,"cache_read_input_tokens":19289,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":89430},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"462c7d97-6a8b-4cbd-afda-e9c6e06e5368","uuid":"4416e315-8af0-44f2-9328-a59ff42740dc"}
